  tk.sv  § genCaDirectRefBindsGenLocal (ModuleEmit, the gen-scope cont_assign
        emitter, just before cas->push_back).  In an ELABORATED gen scope a
        cont_assign ref that IS the WHOLE Lhs or the WHOLE Rhs binds its
        gen-LOCAL VARIABLE; the same name read from INSIDE an operation or a
        select does not, and a PROCESS never does.  This is the finer cut the
        "a blanket bind demoted 10 tests" note in ExprEmit's NamedValue arm
        was missing — that experiment bound EVERY ref in the scope.  Matrix
        (tk.sv, one gen_scope per row, both views):
            assign st[k+1] = va_round;   Rhs whole ref, same scope  -> BOUND
            assign vb_xor  = st[k];      Lhs whole ref, same scope  -> BOUND
                                         Rhs select index          -> unbound
            assign vb_sbox = ~vb_xor;    Lhs, ANCESTOR gen scope    -> BOUND
                                         Rhs under op 4            -> unbound
            assign arr_o   = vd_arr;     Rhs whole ref, UNPACKED
                                         ARRAY, same scope         -> BOUND
            assign sc_o    = vd_sc;      Rhs whole ref, ANCESTOR    -> unbound
            always_comb va_round = ...;  process Lhs                -> unbound
        DISTINGUISHER, measured as a REGRESSION and then gated: the first cut
        allowed an ancestor scope on BOTH sides and moved u2r
        MemoryInGenblock from CMP_EQUAL to CMP_NOTEQUAL — its
        `assign data_buf_c = data_buf_a;` reads an ANCESTOR gen scope's
        unpacked array from a nested if-gen.  tk rows D1/D2 split the two
        candidate causes and it is the SCOPE, not the array-ness.
        Corpus: the prim_present / prim_prince TopModules heads — the FIRST
        movement in that section in seven passes.  prim_present ADVANCED
