PASS-19 OPS NOTES
  * READ THE GENERATED Compare BEFORE THEORIZING.  `NO CHILD REPRODUCES IT`
    with a clean child table means a SCALAR of the node itself:
    ~/git-repos/UHDM/build/generated/src/<type>.cpp is the authority on which
    scalars are compared and in what order, and the bisector's r value is
    literally that scalar subtraction.  Two heads fell out of one grep.
  * A HEAD-LINE DIFF *AND* A failLhs/failRhs DIFF CAN BOTH BE BLIND.  Pass 17
    added failLhs/failRhs to hdiff because head lines alone hid an advance;
    pass 19 hit the next layer — a law that moved the failure one field DEEPER
    inside the same first-failing pair prints nothing in either.  The only
    honest check for "did my law land" is to RE-RUN THE BISECTOR on the head.
  * A REGRESSION IS THE CHEAPEST DISTINGUISHER, and per-law gate rounds are
    what make it cheap.  Law 5 was gated alone precisely so its one regression
    named its own missing condition; a combined round would have cost a bisect.
  * TWO HEADS ON ONE OBJECT REMAIN THE BEST ARITHMETIC ON THE BOARD.  Three of
    this pass's six closures came from two such pairs (sha2/hmac shared the
    LAW-S alias question; prim_generic_pad_wrapper/padring share the
    cont_assign object itself).

